## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic concurrency primitives, memory ordering, and a practical IPC primitive from first principles.

## Key Concepts
- Bounded ring buffer fundamentals
- Atomic indices and memory ordering
- Data visibility vs. race-free updates
- Simple correctness testing under contention

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo C-like sketch
#define N 1024
int buf[N];
atomic_size_t head = 0, tail = 0;

int enqueue(int v){
  size_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  size_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&head, memory_order_acquire);
  size_t next = (t + 1) % N;
  if (next == h) return -1; // full
  buf[t] = v;
  atomic_store_explicit(&tail, next, memory_order_release);
  return 0;
}

int dequeue(int *out){
  size_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&head, memory_order_relaxed);
  size_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&tail, memory_order_acquire);
  if (h == t) return -1; // empty
  *out = buf[h];
  atomic_store_explicit(&head, (h + 1) % N, memory_order_release);
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers/consumers?
- How would you detect and recover from stack/memory ordering mistakes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of process lifecycle, waitpid usage, and zombie avoidance in a practical multi-child scenario.

## Key Concepts
- waitpid semantics and looping
- SIGCHLD handling and reaping
- WNOHANG and non-blocking variants
- Zombie processes and edge cases (stopped/continued)

## Code Example
```javascript
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>

int main() {
  // spawn N children ...
  int finished = 0; int status;
  while (finished < N) {
    pid_t pid = waitpid(-1, &status, 0);
    if (pid > 0) { finished++; /* process status */ }
    else if (pid == -1 && errno == ECHILD) break;
  }
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for SIGCHLD storms or large N?
- What are the risks of ignoring zombie cleanup in long-running daemons?

