## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of OS file locking, race conditions, deadlock avoidance, and IO visibility in a beginner-friendly context.

## Key Concepts
- Advisory locks with fcntl
- F_SETLKW vs F_SETLK
- Deadlock prevention by fixed lock order
- fsync for visibility

## Code Example
```c
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <string.h>

static int lock_log(int fd) {
  struct flock fl; memset(&fl, 0, sizeof(fl)); fl.l_type = F_WRLCK; fl.l_whence = SEEK_SET; fl.l_start = 0; fl.l_len = 0; return fcntl(fd, F_SETLKW, &fl);
}
static int unlock_log(int fd) {
  struct flock fl; memset(&fl, 0, sizeof(fl)); fl.l_type = F_UNLCK; fl.l_whence = SEEK_SET; fl.l_start = 0; fl.l_len = 0; return fcntl(fd, F_SETLK, &fl);
}

void safe_log_write(int fd, const char *msg) {
  if (lock_log(fd) == -1) return;
  write(fd, msg, strlen(msg));
  fsync(fd);
  unlock_log(fd);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you support concurrent readers without writer starvation?
- What changes for non-advisory or cross-language compatibility?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic demand paging, synchronization, and a tiny per-page state machine.

## Key Concepts
- Demand paging
- Per-page locking
- State machine: EMPTY, LOADING, READY
- Wait/wake and memory visibility

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: per-page fault path in a tiny OS
typedef enum { EMPTY, LOADING, READY } page_state_t;
typedef struct { page_state_t s; spinlock l; char *data; bool dirty; } page_t;
void handle_fault(page_t *p){
  acquire(&p->l);
  if (p->s==READY){ release(&p->l); return; }
  if (p->s==EMPTY){ p->s=LOADING; release(&p->l); read_disk_async(p); p->s=READY; wake_all(&p->wq); return; }
  while (p->s!=READY) wait(&p->wq, &p->l);
  release(&p->l);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test concurrent touches of the same page to ensure only a single IO is issued?
- How would you extend to multiple pages or prefetch while preserving correctness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of demand paging, synchronization, and TLB consistency in a realistic but simple setup.

## Key Concepts
- Demand paging and lazy page-in
- Per-page locking to serialize concurrent faults
- Correct handling of IO vs zero-fill paths
- Page-table updates and TLB invalidation
- Wakeup semantics for waiters

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal fault handler sketch (C-like)
void handle_fault(Page* p){
  acquire(p->lock);
  if (p->present) { release(p->lock); return; }
  if (p->loading) { wait_on(p); release(p->lock); /* queue waiter */ return; }
  p->loading = true; release(p->lock);
  // IO or zero-fill
  if (has_backing_file(p)) read_file_into(p);
  else zero_fill(p);
  acquire(p->lock);
  p->loading = false; p->present = true; update_pte(p);
  flush_tlb(p);
  wake_waiters(p);
  release(p->lock);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test race conditions with two faulting threads? 
- How would you handle I/O errors from the backing file? 
- How would this scale to many pages in parallel?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests understanding of inter-process sharing via mmap, page cache semantics, and memory visibility without user-space locking.

## Key Concepts
- MAP_SHARED page mappings
- Page cache dirty bits and writeback
- Cache coherence and TLB effects
- Lightweight synchronization in shared memory (CAS, acquire/release)

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo: writer uses CAS to acquire lock, writes, then releases
uint32_t *lock = (uint32_t*)addr; // shared region
while (__sync_lock_test_and_set(lock, 1)) {}
// write 8 bytes to region after lock
memcpy((void*)(addr+8), data, 8);
__sync_lock_release(lock);

// Reader checks lock before reading
while (*lock) {} // wait for writer
memcpy(data, (void*)(addr+8), 8);
```

## Tradeoffs
Lock: Simple, ensures exclusive access but blocks readers during writes.
Seqlock: Readers can proceed concurrently but must retry if write occurs mid-read; better for read-heavy workloads.
